The client must be able to communicate with the Domain Controller before it can successfully join the domain.

🔍 Phase 7 — DNS Configuration

DNS is a critical component of Active Directory.

Active Directory relies heavily on DNS to locate domain services and Domain Controllers.

The Windows client was configured to use the Domain Controller as its preferred DNS server.

Example:

Preferred DNS Server:

192.168.x.x

where the address represents the Domain Controller.

🧪 DNS Testing

DNS functionality was tested using:

ipconfig /all

This command was used to verify:

IP address
Subnet mask
Default gateway
DNS server

Additional testing:

nslookup ad.domain.com

and:

ping ad.domain.com

Successful name resolution indicates that the client can resolve the Active Directory domain.

🔗 Phase 8 — Joining Windows 11 Client to Domain

The Windows 11 client was joined to the Active Directory domain.

General process:

Configure the client network
Set the Domain Controller as preferred DNS
Verify network connectivity
Open Windows system properties
Select domain membership
Enter the Active Directory domain
Provide authorized domain credentials
Restart the client
Verify domain membership
Test domain login

Example:

Domain:

ad.domain.com
🔐 Phase 9 — Domain Authentication

After joining the Windows client to the domain, domain credentials were used to test authentication.

The login process demonstrates centralized identity management.

Instead of creating separate local accounts on every computer, users can authenticate using their Active Directory accounts.

Example:

Username:

AD\ayush

or:

ayush@ad.domain.com
🖥️ Computer Account Management

When a computer joins an Active Directory domain, a corresponding computer account is created in Active Directory.

The computer account allows the Domain Controller to identify and manage the workstation.

Example:

WIN11-CLIENT$

Computer accounts can be viewed and managed through:

Active Directory Users and Computers

📂 Organizational Units (OUs)

Organizational Units can be used to logically organize Active Directory objects.

Example:

ad.domain.com
│
├── IT
│   ├── Users
│   └── Computers
│
├── HR
│   ├── Users
│   └── Computers
│
└── Support
    ├── Users
    └── Computers

OUs are useful for:

Organizing users
Organizing computers
Applying Group Policies
Delegating administration
Managing departments
👥 Security Groups

Security groups can be used to simplify permission management.

Instead of assigning permissions to individual users, users can be placed into groups.

Example:

IT-Support
│
├── User 1
├── User 2
└── User 3

Permissions can then be assigned to the group rather than each individual account.

🔑 Permissions

The lab environment was used to understand Windows access permissions.

Important concepts include:

NTFS Permissions

Permissions applied to files and folders stored on NTFS volumes.

Common permissions include:

Full Control
Modify
Read & Execute
Read
Write
Share Permissions

Permissions applied when folders are shared over the network.

Understanding both NTFS and Share permissions is important for IT Support and Windows administration.

🛡️ Authentication vs Authorization

A key concept studied during the project was the difference between authentication and authorization.

Authentication

Authentication answers:

"Who are you?"

Example:

Username + Password
Authorization

Authorization answers:

"What are you allowed to access?"

Example:

User belongs to IT-Support group
        ↓
Group has access to shared folder
        ↓
User can access the folder
