🧰 Troubleshooting Methodology

A structured troubleshooting methodology was followed while working with the lab.

Identify the issue
       ↓
Gather information
       ↓
Check physical / virtual connectivity
       ↓
Check IP configuration
       ↓
Check DNS
       ↓
Check Domain Controller
       ↓
Test connectivity
       ↓
Apply corrective action
       ↓
Verify the solution
       ↓
Document the issue

This approach is directly relevant to Help Desk and IT Support environments.

🔧 Common Troubleshooting Scenarios
Scenario 1 — Client Cannot Join Domain
Possible causes
Incorrect DNS server
Incorrect domain name
Client cannot reach Domain Controller
Network adapter problem
Domain Controller unavailable
Incorrect credentials
Troubleshooting

First check:

ipconfig /all

Then test:

ping <Domain-Controller-IP>

Test DNS:

nslookup ad.domain.com

Verify that the client is using the Domain Controller as its DNS server.

Scenario 2 — Domain Login Not Working
Possible causes
Incorrect username
Incorrect password
Account disabled
Account locked
Network connectivity issue
Domain Controller unavailable
Incorrect domain selected
Troubleshooting

Check:

Username
Password
Domain
Account status
Network connectivity
Domain Controller availability
Scenario 3 — DNS Resolution Failure

Commands used:

ipconfig /all
ipconfig /flushdns
ipconfig /renew
nslookup ad.domain.com
ping ad.domain.com

The objective is to determine whether the issue is related to:

DNS configuration
Network configuration
Domain Controller availability
Name resolution
Scenario 4 — Client Has No Network Connectivity

Basic troubleshooting:

ipconfig
ping 127.0.0.1
ping <gateway>
ping <Domain-Controller-IP>

The tests help isolate whether the issue is:

Local TCP/IP stack
        ↓
Network adapter
        ↓
Gateway
        ↓
Domain Controller
